DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       mmpprrootteecctt()  changes  the  access  protections for the calling process’s
       memory pages containing any part of the address range in  the  interval
       [_a_d_d_r, _a_d_d_r+_s_i_z_e-1].  _a_d_d_r must be aligned to a page boundary.

       If the calling process tries to access memory in a manner that violates
       the  protections,  then  the  kernel generates a SSIIGGSSEEGGVV signal for the
       process.

       _p_r_o_t is a combination of the following access  flags:  PPRROOTT__NNOONNEE  or  a
       bitwise OR of the other values in the following list:

       PPRROOTT__NNOONNEE
              The memory cannot be accessed at all.

       PPRROOTT__RREEAADD
              The memory can be read.

       PPRROOTT__WWRRIITTEE
              The memory can be modified.

       PPRROOTT__EEXXEECC
              The memory can be executed.

       PPRROOTT__SSEEMM (since Linux 2.5.7)
              The memory can be used for atomic operations.  This flag was in‐
              troduced  as  part  of  the ffuutteexx(2) implementation (in order to
              guarantee the ability to perform atomic operations  required  by
              commands  such  as  FFUUTTEEXX__WWAAIITT), but is not currently used in on
              any architecture.

       PPRROOTT__SSAAOO (since Linux 2.6.26)
              The memory should have strong access ordering.  This feature  is
              specific to the PowerPC architecture (version 2.06 of the archi‐
              tecture specification adds the SAO CPU feature, and it is avail‐
              able on POWER 7 or PowerPC A2, for example).

       Additionally  (since  Linux  2.6.0), _p_r_o_t can have one of the following
       flags set:

       PPRROOTT__GGRROOWWSSUUPP
              Apply the protection mode up to the end of a mapping that  grows
              upwards.   (Such  mappings are created for the stack area on ar‐
              chitectures—for example, HP‐PARISC—that have an upwardly growing
              stack.)

       PPRROOTT__GGRROOWWSSDDOOWWNN
              Apply the protection mode down to the  beginning  of  a  mapping
              that  grows  downward (which should be a stack segment or a seg‐
              ment mapped with the MMAAPP__GGRROOWWSSDDOOWWNN flag set).

       Like mmpprrootteecctt(), ppkkeeyy__mmpprrootteecctt() changes the protection  on  the  pages
