VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       POSIX says that the behavior of mmpprrootteecctt() is unspecified if it is  ap‐
       plied to a region of memory that was not obtained via mmmmaapp(2).

       On Linux, it is always permissible to call mmpprrootteecctt() on any address in
       a  process’s  address  space (except for the kernel vsyscall area).  In
       particular, it can be used to  change  existing  code  mappings  to  be
       writable.

       Whether  PPRROOTT__EEXXEECC  has  any effect different from PPRROOTT__RREEAADD depends on
       processor architecture, kernel version, and process state.  If RREEAADD__IIMM‐‐
       PPLLIIEESS__EEXXEECC is set in the process’s  personality  flags  (see  ppeerrssoonnaall‐‐
       iittyy(2)), specifying PPRROOTT__RREEAADD will implicitly add PPRROOTT__EEXXEECC.

       On   some  hardware  architectures  (e.g.,  i386),  PPRROOTT__WWRRIITTEE  implies
       PPRROOTT__RREEAADD.

       POSIX.1 says that an implementation may permit access other  than  that
       specified  in  _p_r_o_t,  but  at  a minimum can allow write access only if
       PPRROOTT__WWRRIITTEE has been set, and must not allow any access if PPRROOTT__NNOONNEE has
       been set.

       Applications should be  careful  when  mixing  use  of  mmpprrootteecctt()  and
       ppkkeeyy__mmpprrootteecctt().   On  x86,  when  mmpprrootteecctt()  is used with _p_r_o_t set to
       PPRROOTT__EEXXEECC a pkey may be allocated and set on the memory  implicitly  by
       the kernel, but only when the pkey was 0 previously.

       On  systems that do not support protection keys in hardware, ppkkeeyy__mmpprroo‐‐
       tteecctt() may still be used, but _p_k_e_y must be set to -1.  When called this
       way, the operation of ppkkeeyy__mmpprrootteecctt() is equivalent to mmpprrootteecctt().

